# AWS End-to-End CI/CD Pipeline – Project Details and Troubleshooting Notes

This document contains a complete description of how the AWS end-to-end CI/CD pipeline was implemented, along with the real issues faced during setup and the exact troubleshooting steps taken to resolve them.

The purpose of this file is to document the learning journey, mistakes, and fixes encountered while building the pipeline.

---

PROJECT OVERVIEW

The goal of this project was to build a fully automated CI/CD pipeline on AWS to deploy a Python Flask application packaged as a Docker container onto an Amazon EC2 instance.

The pipeline automates the following process:
- Detect code changes from GitHub
- Build a Docker image
- Push the image to Docker Hub
- Deploy the updated application to EC2 using AWS CodeDeploy

The project includes both Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Deployment (CD).

---

ARCHITECTURE AND SERVICES USED

Source Control:
GitHub is used to store the Flask application source code, Dockerfile, deployment scripts, and configuration files.

Continuous Integration:
AWS CodeBuild is used to build the Docker image and push it to Docker Hub.

Continuous Deployment:
AWS CodeDeploy is used to deploy the application onto the EC2 instance using lifecycle hooks defined in appspec.yml.

Pipeline Orchestration:
AWS CodePipeline connects GitHub, CodeBuild, and CodeDeploy into a single automated pipeline.

Target Environment:
Amazon EC2 (Ubuntu) is used as the deployment server, running Docker containers.

---

APPLICATION AND REPOSITORY SETUP

A simple Python Flask application was used.

The following files were added at the root of the GitHub repository:

- appspec.yml  
This file is required by CodeDeploy and defines where files should be copied and which scripts should run during deployment.

- start_container.sh  
This script pulls the latest Docker image from Docker Hub and starts the container on EC2.

- stop_container.sh  
This script stops and removes any running container to avoid port conflicts during redeployment.

These scripts ensure that every deployment starts with a clean application state.

---

EC2 INSTANCE SETUP

An Ubuntu EC2 instance was launched to host the application.

The following configurations were performed:
- Public IP enabled
- Internet Gateway attached to the VPC
- Route table updated with a 0.0.0.0/0 route
- Docker installed
- CodeDeploy agent installed manually
- EC2 instance tagged for CodeDeploy targeting

Example EC2 tag used:
Key: Name  
Value: sample-python-app

---

IAM ROLES CONFIGURATION

Three IAM roles were required for the pipeline to work correctly.

EC2 INSTANCE ROLE

This role was attached to the EC2 instance and included the following permissions:
- AmazonEC2RoleforAWSCodeDeploy
- AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess

This role allows the EC2 instance to communicate with CodeDeploy and download deployment artifacts from Amazon S3.

CODEDEPLOY SERVICE ROLE

This role was created with:
- Trusted service: codedeploy.amazonaws.com
- Policy attached: AWSCodeDeployRole

This role allows CodeDeploy to manage deployment operations.

CODEPIPELINE ROLE

This role is used by CodePipeline to trigger deployments.
Initially, the pipeline failed because this role did not have permission to create deployments.

The issue was fixed by attaching:
- AWSCodeDeployFullAccess

---

CODEDEPLOY CONFIGURATION

A CodeDeploy application was created using the EC2/On-Premises platform.

A deployment group was created with the following configuration:
- Deployment type: In-place
- EC2 instance selection based on tags
- CodeDeploy service role attached

The deployment group links the application to the correct EC2 instance.

---

CODEPIPELINE CONFIGURATION

The pipeline was configured with the following stages:

Source Stage:
- GitHub connected using AWS CodeConnections

Build Stage:
- AWS CodeBuild builds the Docker image
- The image is pushed to Docker Hub

Deploy Stage:
- AWS CodeDeploy deploys the application to EC2 using appspec.yml

Once configured, any push to GitHub automatically triggers the entire pipeline.

---

ERRORS FACED AND TROUBLESHOOTING

SSH CONNECTION TIMEOUT

Issue:
Unable to SSH into the EC2 instance.

Cause:
- Internet Gateway not attached
- Route table missing default internet route

Fix:
- Attached Internet Gateway to VPC
- Added 0.0.0.0/0 route
- Enabled public IP for EC2

---

IAM ROLE NOT VISIBLE FOR EC2

Issue:
IAM role was not visible while attaching it to the EC2 instance.

Cause:
The role was created for CodeDeploy instead of EC2.

Fix:
Recreated the IAM role using EC2 as the trusted service.

---

CODEPIPELINE AUTHORIZATION ERROR

Error:
codedeploy:CreateDeployment is not authorized

Cause:
CodePipeline IAM role lacked CodeDeploy permissions.

Fix:
Attached AWSCodeDeployFullAccess to the CodePipeline role.

---

DEPLOYMENT FAILED WITH HEALTH_CONSTRAINTS

Issue:
Deployment failed with HEALTH_CONSTRAINTS error.

Cause:
The deployment lifecycle failed on the EC2 instance because the application bundle could not be downloaded.

---

DOWNLOADBUNDLE FAILED (UNKNOWNERROR)

Issue:
CodeDeploy failed during the DownloadBundle phase.

Cause:
EC2 IAM role did not have permission to read artifacts from S3.

Fix:
Attached AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess to the EC2 IAM role and restarted the CodeDeploy agent.

---

DOCKER PORT BINDING ISSUE

Issue:
Deployment failed because the Docker container was already running on the required port.

Cause:
Old container was not stopped before starting a new one.

Fix:
Implemented stop_container.sh to stop and remove existing containers before starting a new deployment.

---

VERIFICATION

After successful deployment, the application was verified using:

docker ps

The Docker container was confirmed to be running successfully, and the application was accessible using the EC2 public IP.

---

KEY LEARNINGS

- IAM misconfiguration is a common cause of CI/CD failures
- CodeDeploy agent must be running on the EC2 instance
- appspec.yml must be placed at the root of the repository
- EC2 instances require S3 read access for deployment bundles
- Cleaning up old containers is necessary for stable deployments
- Real DevOps learning happens through debugging and troubleshooting

